From c77156b2aed85b6895a8fc9f3fe143bd0dcf62ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Jezl <john@jezl.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 09:36:38 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] bench stealing --assert-shares and bench idle-wakes (#1216)

Turn the sustained bench's per-CPU distribution into a pass/fail check,
and measure the idle wake rate that a wake-on-push change costs.

`bench stealing --sustain [ms] --assert-shares`: CPU 1 is the owner and
every other online CPU a thief. The run fails, printing one
`ASSERT-SHARES: FAIL ...` line (the lowest thief and its share, the
best thief and its share, every thief below half) and returning 1 from
the shell command, when a thief's share is below half the best thief's,
when no thief executed a task, or when the run timed out. Otherwise it
prints `ASSERT-SHARES: PASS`. The decision is steal_shares_check(), a
pure static inline in kernel/sched/steal_trace.h, with a regression
suite in kernel/tests/test_steal_shares.c (even shares, a thief at 49%
of the best, exactly half, odd rounding, owner excluded, ties, a thief
at 0, timeout, no thief). `--assert-shares` without `--sustain` is
refused: a 16-task burst is too short for a share to mean anything.

`bench idle-wakes [ms]` (default 5000) snapshots sched_diag_idle_loops[]
and sched_diag_tick[] per CPU, sleeps the shell so CPU 0 idles too, and
prints one `iw mode=rest` line per CPU with idle-loop passes and ticks
per second. Each idle-loop pass is one return from the wait
instruction, so idle_per_s is the idle wake rate. A sustained run
prints the same `iw mode=sustained` lines from its own snapshots (the
idle= and ticks= deltas the c2t lines already carry, as rates).

On jetson-nano-2, coherency run 3 measured CPUs 2 and 3 at 0.397 to
0.830 of the best thief's median share with the NC kernel
(F-nc-instr-uefi-run01..20.txt, C-nc-instr-kexec-run01..20.txt on
gauntlet-3-hw-jetson, critic-3-C2.md section 3), and at 0 tasks in
three runs of the NC_AXIS=OFF cell (A-ncoff-instr-kexec-run06, 15, 20).

Docs: docs/work-stealing-bench.md (new section), help.c, shell fact
sheet.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
